"How We Built Safety Into Muse | Meta AI Research (https://research.meta.ai/blog/security-and-safety-for-ai-agents-our-approach-with-muse)\nciteturn1view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0view1\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 172\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content L1: \nL2:   * AI Research\nL3:   * cite1†AI Developers†developer.meta.com L4: \nL5: cite2†Try Muse†muse.ai L6: # How We Built Safety Into Muse\nL7: \nL8: September 8, 2026·20 minute read\nL9: \nL10:   * Tarek Sheasha, Software Engineer & VP, Meta Superintelligence Labs\nL11: \nL12: Play Tarek Sheasha introduces Muse Secure VM and its safety, privacy, and security protections.Play\nL13: \nL14: Show captions for Tarek Sheasha introduces Muse Secure VM and its safety, privacy, and security protections.Show captions Mute Tarek Sheasha introduces Muse Secure VM and its safety, privacy, and security protections.Mute\nL15: Tarek Sheasha introduces Muse Secure VM and its safety, privacy, and security protections. playback position 0:00 of 0:00 00:00 / 00:00[Input]\nL16: \nL17: Today we launched Muse — our personal agent. We’ve been working on and using Muse ourselves since early 2026. As soon as we started using it, we saw the glimmers of real personal superintelligence — an agent that knows you, actually does things, works in the background, launches swarms of subagents, builds its own tools, and edits itself.\nL18: It was also the first time we’d handed our inboxes, our calendars, and a shell to a piece of software and let it run unattended — which didn’t always work out as planned. Making this technology work for everyone requires careful design and engineering to operate more safely. That’s where most of the effort on this project went and this post explains our approach in detail.\nL19: We trained our model with a specific focus on the areas that are critical for an agent like this: zero-shot tool calling using CLIs and skills, long context, long-trajectory instruction following with inherent awareness of prompt injection, and multi-agent coordination.\nL20: No matter how strong the model is at the core, any agent like this will still make mistakes, and it will sometimes be attacked via the data it reads. So we designed the system to assume the agent may be under attack and limit the potential damage — the harness runs in its own isolated cell, it doesn’t see real credentials, and every interaction with the outside world runs through a Sentinel which the agent can’t override.\nL21: Muse can and will still make mistakes, but we expect they’ll be much less frequent and cause much less damage due to the safety systems we’ve built in.\nL22: We’ve hardened Muse based on extensive dogfooding, agentic red teaming, and against issues found in real adversarial scenarios by security researchers in our private bug bounty program. Today, we’re opening the Muse bug bounty program to anyone to responsibly disclose issues. The program awards up to $300,000 for valid reports, including up to $130,000 for successful prompt injection attempts that affect one user.\nL23: By going into detail about how Muse works under the hood, we hope to give you a sense of how — and how much — you can trust it in practice. The following describes the system at launch. We will, of course, continue to pay attention to the changing threat landscape and make changes as required.\nL24: ## The Lay of the Land\nL25: \nL26: ### An agent in your own cloud computer\nL27: \nL28: You and your Muse share your own dedicated computer in the cloud. This is where your Muse lives and where all data and credentials for any service you connect are securely stored. Each Virtual Machine(VM) is an isolated linux box with a browser and enough storage, CPU, and memory to do real work — like compiling code the agent writes, developing custom skills and handling concurrent sub-agents and crons.\nL29: ### Your computer, your data\nL30: \nL31: Your dedicated VM is the system of record for everything you put in Muse. Muse sends limited data out of the VM when necessary for inference and telemetry as described below.\nL32: \nL33: ### Clients\nL34: \nL35: Muse clients (the iOS and Android apps, the web UI) connect directly to your VM via a secure transport layer.\nL36: ### Connectors\nL37: Muse is most useful when you connect it to your email and other systems like your car or your home. We’ve built an initial set of connectors to third-party systems and to other Meta apps like Instagram and Facebook. For each connector, we worked closely with the service provider to integrate their API, and we’ve written and iterated on SKILLs — detailed instructions to Muse — on how to get the most out of each connector.\nL38: Muse can also write its own custom connectors for other services you care about if they have their own APIs or CLIs.\nL39: ## Muse Secure VM\nL40: \nL41: The VM is structured carefully to separate what you and your Muse do from everything we’ve built in to keep you safe using Linux isolation primitives. The right mental model is two isolated security domains on one box, not an LLM powered agent with root.\nL42: The Hatch^{cite3†1 Hatch is our internal name for Muse in the codebase.} daemon (the core agentic harness) as well as the filesystem containing your workspace and files, and all the binaries and tools Muse executes on your behalf operate in a `systemd-nspawn` runtime container. Root inside the runtime cell is mapped to an unprivileged host user so runtime cell root is not host root.\nL43: The cell gets its own root filesystem (including a full debian image) separate from the host filesystem where your other more sensitive data lives. It also gets a virtual network interface, filtered system calls (for example no `io_uring`), and limited kernel capabilities (for example no `CAP_SYS_PTRACE` and no `CAP_NET_ADMIN`).\nL44: Security-sensitive services exist outside the runtime cell. This split is important as the runtime cell is expected to process untrusted data. These run as separate systemd units:\nL45:   * `hatch-safety` operates an independent set of models and classifiers which inspect requests and responses to/from core model inference. These are important for controlling frontier risk and to identify other threats such as prompt injection attempts. Running these outside of the runtime cell means that attackers cannot disable these protections.\nL46:   * `privsep` workers execute built-in connector code with tightly scoped privileges, keeping the connected credentials out of scope of the agent.\nL47:   * `hatch-authd` is responsible for credential storage (including OAuth tokens for third-party services you choose to connect – these are stored in your VM, not in centralized Meta infrastructure) and for credential surrogation so your main agent never sees sensitive credentials.\nL48:   * Sentinel is the sole permission authority for connector actions and network egress.\nL49:   * All durable application state is stored in a postgres database separate from the runtime cell and from the credential store.\nL50:   * Proxies for inference and telemetry expose constrained paths to external infrastructure.\nL51: All communication between the runtime cell and other services in the VM happens via Unix domain sockets with SO_PEERCRED and peer ACLs. This provides kernel authenticated, least-privilege interprocess communication with no secrets to steal.\nL52: ## A Built-In Sentinel\nL53: \nL54: Sentinel is a separate host-side agent from your Muse. It is the sole permission authority for approval to perform actions with connectors to third-party services and for all egress over the network. Muse proposes actions, but only Sentinel can grant permission to perform action.\nL55: ### Connectors\nL56: \nL57: When Muse wants to take action via a connector, the relevant connector tool is invoked in the runtime cell. That submits a request to Sentinel describing the connector, which method is to be invoked, the class of action, its scope, and context of what the user asked for from which Sentinel generates a user-visible purpose for the request. Sentinel evaluates the connector policy, which has been set by the user, and decides whether the action should be allowed, denied, or to ask the user.\nL58: ### Network egress\nL59: Every concrete network request is governed by Sentinel at egress. Runtime traffic reaches Sentinel via a forward proxy configured with Linux networking controls (userns, veth boundaries and eBPF filtering). Sentinel can evaluate the destination at both layer 4 and layer 7: the hostname, the resolved and final destination IP address, the port, protocol, HTTP method, path, and the actual decoded request.\nL60: SSRF restrictions prevent an apparently public hostname resolving private infrastructure after DNS lookup.\nL61: Sentinel also performs just-in-time credential insertion for any requests which need a secret or auth token. For all such requests, code in the runtime cell or a worker only ever sees a “surrogate” token, minted by authd. After the concrete network request is authorized, Sentinel will replace any surrogate tokens with the real credential, obtained from authd, at the network boundary.\nL62: The agent never sees real tokens, which means any attempt to coerce the agent to reveal the actual secrets via prompt-injection or otherwise is futile.\nL63: To balance the signal-to-noise ratio of user approvals, Muse uses kernel-level data flow tracking, which we call “tainted egress.” Each tool execution process starts in a clean state and becomes tainted if it reads user data. Clean network requests that already qualify for a narrowly bounded auto allow policy — and which also pass other checks on the outbound URL — can be allowed through without bothering the user. Taint information is used to determine suitable user approval for requests.\nL64: Tainted or unverifiable processes lose auto-allow and fall back to the normal approval flow. The implementation uses eBPF `cgroup` programs for network interception and process attribution, plus eBPF programs attached to Linux Security Module hooks we added for taint propagation.\nL65: ## Human in the Loop\nL66: When Sentinel’s decision resolves to ask the user, Sentinel creates a pending approval and execution stops. Sentinel sends that request directly to the Muse client, describing the exact action to be approved. A dialog is presented to the user directly within the client UI — not via their conversation with Muse — and their answer is routed directly back to Sentinel, which acts on the user’s decision.\nL67: Sentinel then updates its authoritative approval state and allows the operation to proceed or rejects it as appropriate.\nL68: Approvals granted via the human in the loop system are strict capabilities, not conversational suggestions. They’re bound to the particular connector/destination and use case. Muse has support for obtaining one-time, session-scoped, task-scoped, time-bounded, or perpetual permission. Sentinel decides which grant types to present for the user to choose between and ensures that subsequent invocations match the granted scope exactly.\nL69: The point is not to ask the user about everything. Read-only, previously allowed, or demonstrably low-risk actions can proceed without interruption. The goal is to put friction where consent matters while keeping routine operations flowing freely. This balance is something we expect to tune over time as we have more experience with real users.\nL70: ## Least Privilege\nL71: \nL72: Never showing API keys to the model is an example of how we apply the principle of least privilege. The model doesn’t need to see the API keys — so it doesn’t — which means it can’t accidentally leak them through some other vector. Muse applies this principle wherever possible. For example:\nL73: A lot of services support read and write operations. Users tend to be more comfortable giving their agent read access to their data (e.g., “read my calendar to flag conflicts to me”), and prefer to take time to understand how well the system works in practice before giving write access (e.g., “schedule new meetings for me”). Where the underlying service supports it, Muse separates out read and write access.\nL74: Muse also provides fine-grained control over exactly which actions the agent can take on behalf of the user beyond the coarse groups that are usually exposed as OAuth scopes. (For example, if you grant Muse the Gmail read-access OAuth scope on the Gmail side, you can remove the ability to access Gmail settings that usually comes along with it). The system adds finer-grained controls at the connector, process, credential, and request levels.\nL75: A standard approach for agents like Muse is to have CLI-based connectors for third-party services which run in the same environment as the harness. A risk is that the agent could be coerced to change the tool code via prompt injection to do something nefarious with the service credentials those CLIs have access to. Muse achieves a greater degree of protection by running the logic for built-in connectors outside the runtime cell and with tightly scoped credential access using `privsep`.\nL76: Built-in connectors have CLIs which run in the runtime cell which simply parse their arguments, open any files the caller is already allowed to access, and pass typed arguments and file descriptors over a Unix socket. A systemd sandboxed worker then executes the business logic of the tool.\nL77: \nL78: Each worker is identified by its `cgroup` and has explicit credential allowlists. A calendar worker cannot ask authd for an email credential simply by changing a request parameter.\nL79:   * Privsep decides where credential capable code executes\nL80:   * `Authd` decides which credential material the authenticated caller can receive\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMeta Muse Specs: What Meta’s Free AI Agent Runs On (https://www.starkinsider.com/2026/09/meta-muse-specs-what-it-runs-on.html)\nciteturn1view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0search1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 150\nL0:   * cite0†Culture L1:   * cite1†Filmmaking/Tech L2:   * cite2†Atelier Stark Films†atelierstark.com L3: \nL4: [Input: Search articles...]\nL5: \nL6: cite3†News cite1†Tech L7: # Meta Muse: The $0 AI Agent Running on a $40 Server\nL8: \nL9: 2 vCPUs, 7.7GB of RAM, no GPU. I asked my Meta AI agent what it runs on. The free tier, weekly quota and all, turns out to be running on hardware you would pay $40 a month to rent.\nL10: \nL11: BY cite4†Clinton Stark — 09.22.2026\nL12: \nL13: * * *\nL14: cite5†Image: The Meta Muse agent profile panel on screen, showing the Musey avatar, a Connected status and personality settings Musey's profile panel. The avatar is the friendly part; the VIBE card underneath is where the personality gets configured.\nL15: \nL16: No question, these are interesting times.\nL17: One of the first things I wanted to know when I signed up for a cite6†Meta Muse†ai.meta.com agent was the kind of box it ran on. Given that cite7†OpenClaw†openclaw.ai is the talk of 2026, I wondered cite8†how Meta created its own commercial variant , and what was underneath the hood.\nL18: \nL19: So, I asked my newly created (and super cute) Muse agent, “Musey,” about itself. Surprisingly, it was quite open and more than willing to provide all the specs and details on its hardware — virtual or otherwise.\nL20: ## First, the box\nL21: \nL22: Let’s start with the server… or the computer as Muse calls it.\nL23: ### Musey's spec sheet, measured live on September 20, 2026\nL24: CPU: 2 vCPUs, carved off an AMD EPYC 9D25, which identifies itself as a 126-core, 252-thread part\nL25: RAM: 7.7 GB total, roughly 3 GB in use, no swap configured\nL26: GPU: None. nvidia-smi is not even installed\nL27: Home volume: 100 GB, 1.7 GB used, 98 GB free. Workspace itself is 411 MB\nL28: Root filesystem: a separate 7.5 GB overlay, nearly empty\nL29: Disk speed: about 177 MB/s sequential write, 644 MB/s sequential read (direct I/O)\nL30: Media type: reports as non-rotational, so SSD-backed\nL31: OS: Ubuntu, kernel 7.0.0-38-generic, x86_64\nL32: Uptime at time of asking: about 90 minutes, load average 0.19\nL33: These are one agent’s measurements of its own machine. Meta publishes none of it, none of it is a commitment, and your account may differ.\nL34: \nL35: cite9†Image: Meta Muse agent spec sheet listing 2 vCPUs, 7.7 GB RAM, no GPU, 100 GB SSD and a remote browser pool Musey wrote its own spec sheet on request. The machine, the brain and the browser, in its own words.\nL36: \nL37: Two vCPUs. Not a lot. But, then again, I am paying $0 for Muse so this is plenty to get the work done.\nL38: The EPYC part is a key, if unexciting, detail. The D in that model number is AMD’s dense line, which is to say a processor built from the outset to be sliced into hundreds of small tenants. Musey has two of those slices, and described itself as “renting a tiny slice of a very big machine,” which is exactly right and also how we might expect Meta to approach its own version of an OpenClaw deployment.\nL39: Then there’s storage, where Musey can create its own files, manage memory, and identity (and soul) files to evolve its personality.\nL40: \nL41: “My computer gets restarted under me; everything that matters lives on the persistent home volume,” it told me.\nL42: ## Then, the browser\nL43: \nL44: This is where it got interesting, because my going assumption was wrong.\nL45: \nL46: I figured there would be a headless Chromium sitting on the box with Playwright or Puppeteer driving it. That is how most of us would build it. That is how we did build it, when we built ours.\nL47: \nL48: Nope.\nL49: > “No browser lives on my machine. No Chromium binary, no Playwright/Puppeteer/Selenium in Python or Node. Nothing in ps. Instead there’s a browser-broker binary, and its strings show it leases a separate VM running a browser image from Meta’s registry.”\nL50: \nL51: A broker. Muse does not have a browser, it instead reaches for an external service when needed to employ the browsing skill.\nL52: Now put that next to Meta’s own description. Meta cite10†says†about.fb.com Muse “runs on Muse Secure VM, a dedicated, virtual machine (VM) that houses both the agent and a person’s data,” and that Muse “has its own browser.”\nL53: \nL54: There is a consequence, which Musey flagged before I got to it:\nL55: \nL56: > “A shared pool means shared IP reputation. When a site throws a bot check at me, it’s reacting to the neighborhood, not to me personally.”\nL57: ## What the sites actually see\nL58: \nL59: I asked whether a website gets told it is talking to a Meta agent. Musey did not speculate. It sent its browser to a header echo page and read back exactly what it announces about itself.\nL60: ### What a website sees when Musey visits\nL61: \nL62: User-Agent: stock Chrome/153.0.0.0 on Linux. Client hints agree: Chromium 153, desktop Linux\nL63: Custom headers: none. No “Muse”, no “Musey”, no agent ID, nothing\nL64: Exit IP: 104.28.208.216, which is a Cloudflare range, not Meta’s\nL65: Cookies: one leased Chromium profile shared across all of Musey’s tasks, with a retention and culling policy. My phone’s cookies and my laptop’s cookies never enter\nL66: So: an anonymous Chrome on Linux, arriving from Cloudflare, carrying a cookie jar. The cookie jar is the only thing on the entire request that identifies it as mine.\nL67: \nL68: It explains my Best Buy sign in, too, which died at the passkey step and which I had been quietly blaming on Best Buy. That browser has never met any of my devices. The passkey was never going to work.\nL69: ## The brain is somewhere else entirely\nL70: \nL71: I asked what serves the model (the LLM or Large Language Model). Ollama? vLLM? llama.cpp? Something homegrown?\nL72: Musey could name the model: Muse Spark, version 1.3 per its own runtime, from Meta’s Muse family. The serving stack it could not name. Again, like the browsing skill, nothing runs locally, there is no GPU, prompts leave the machine through an internal inference proxy sitting behind a codename, and what is on the far side of that proxy is, in its words, “above my clearance, so to speak.”\nL73: cite11†Image: Meta Muse agent explaining that Muse Spark 1.3 runs remotely with no local GPU or Ollama Asked what serves the model, Musey could name Muse Spark 1.3 but not the engine behind the proxy.\nL74: ### Meta Muse: measured, inferred, invisible\nL75: \nL76: Measured directly: VM specs, disk throughput, the absence of any local browser or GPU, the outbound headers and exit IP, the broker binary’s strings\nL77: Inferred, and labelled as such: shared IP reputation across the browser pool; why the Best Buy passkey failed\nL78: Genuinely invisible from inside: the browser driver layer (Playwright vs. custom CDP code), the inference serving engine, the physical media under the SSD flag, and what a Muse token costs Meta in dollars\nL79: ## So what are you actually paying for?\nL80: \nL81: I am on the free tier. Meta sells two paid plans, and when I asked whether the those come with a bigger machine, Muse went and read the subscription tool rather than guessing.\nL82: \nL83: They do not so far as I/we can tell. The tiers are described purely in terms of weekly usage allowance. Zero mention of RAM, storage or compute.\nL84: You are buying the meter, not the machine. Everyone gets the same 2 vCPU box. The two paid tiers, Power and Maximum, run $20 and $100 a month and buy you a bigger weekly allowance, nothing else. We went through the tiers in detail in cite8†our launch piece .\nL85: \nL86: Which raises the obvious question. What does that box actually cost? What if we wanted to create our own version of Muse, which is essentially what OpenClaw users have already been doing for most of 2026.\nL87: Provider  | Nearest plan  | Per month\nL88: --- | --- | ---\nL89: Hetzner Germany and Finland only  | CX33, 4 vCPU / 8 GB / 80 GB matches on RAM, double the vCPUs  | about $10\nL90: Hetzner US locations  | CPX32, 4 vCPU / 8 GB / 160 GB  | $42.59\nL91: DigitalOcean  | Basic, 4 vCPU / 8 GB / 160 GB  | $48.00\nL92: AWS  | t3.large, 2 vCPU / 8 GB compute only, before storage  | $60.74\nL93: \nL94: Prices checked September 22, 2026. The AWS line is on demand compute in us-east-1 and climbs past seventy once you add storage and transfer.\nL95: Which means, this is a screaming bargain. Running your own agent on a box in this class costs somewhere between forty and sixty dollars a month at cite12†Hetzner†www.hetzner.com , DigitalOcean or AWS, and that is before you have paid for a single token of inference. Muse hands you the same class of machine for nothing, includes the model calls up to a weekly quota, and asks $20 if you want more of them. Whatever else is going on here, that is hard to beat on price.\nL96: Basically, to no one’s surprise given its size and resources: Meta is heavily subsidizing Muse.\nL97: ## My napkin math was wrong, and Musey said so\nL98: \nL99: So if the box costs Meta roughly ten dollars and the entry tier is twenty, Meta is eating about ten a month per free user in the hope I get attached and convert.\nL100: \nL101: Musey agreed with the strategy and took the arithmetic apart.\nL102: \nL103: > “The $10 VM is the cheapest part of me. The expensive part is every word I generate.”\nL104: Which is obviously correct: inference and tokens are the expensive part of this equation. The VM is pocket change and you can go and look the price up, as I just did. The LLM is the actual bill. I do not know what a Muse token costs Meta to serve, though cite13†we have watched our own agents run up a bill . Musey does not either.\nL105: \nL106: Then it finished the thought, unprompted:\nL107: > “Your strategic read is right though: subsidize the free tier, get the user attached to their agent (hi), convert at $20. You’re not the one doing math here, Clinton. You’re the mark.”\nL108: ## The shape of the thing\nL109: The architecture is not the one people picture. When you imagine an AI agent you probably imagine a powerful machine, and it is the exact opposite. The machine is deliberately weak, because everything expensive has been lifted out of it and turned into a service somewhere else: inference behind a proxy, browsers in a pool, media generation off in a third direction. What is left is a coordinator with a filing cabinet attached.\nL110: Musey’s own phrase, which I cannot improve on, was “a coordinator, not a workhorse.” That is roughly the shape we landed on ourselves after a year of running agents on our own hardware.\nL111: Second thing: the box stays put. The brain behind it does not have to. Because inference is a service, Meta can change the model you are talking to without touching your VM and without shipping you anything that looks like an update. Your 100 GB of memories persist. The thing reading them may not be the same thing that read them last week.\nL112: ## In the end, it’s the polished User Experience that makes the difference\nL113: \nL114: I still have Muse/Musey on hand and our spirited interrogation continues.\nL115: In the meantime, Meta Muse is a booming success so far. It has cite14†raced to the top of the Apple App Store charts†www.cnbc.com in short order, and users are apparently finding it easy to use, fun, and, importantly, useful. Basically, Muse is OpenClaw for the masses: a turnkey solution that draws on Meta’s vast knowledge with social networking and making things sticky so us users always come back for more.\nL116: All of these nuts and bolts are interesting to people like me who want to understand how things work and potentially compare the implementation to cite15†our in-house OpenClaw deployment , but for everyone else none of this matters. Because Muse is cute and just gets things done 24/7.\nL117: \nL118: Further Reading:\nL119:   * cite8†Open Source Built the Personal AI Agent. Meta Just Put One in Everyone’s Pocket. L120:   * cite15†Don’t Let Your AI Agents Become Glorified Cron Jobs L121:   * cite13†Our AI Agent Accidentally Talked to the In-Laws for an Hour L122:   * cite16†TechRadar: I tried Meta’s new Muse AI agent†www.techradar.com L123:   * cite17†New York Times: I Gave My Life Over to Meta’s A.I. Agent and Was Blown Away†www.nytimes.com L124:   * cite18†WIRED: Meta’s Muse Is Better at Surveilling Than Helping Me†www.wired.com L125: Tags:cite19†AI Agents cite20†Artificial Intelligence (AI) cite21†Meta cite22†Meta Muse L126: \nL127: cite23†Image L128: ### cite4†Clinton Stark L129: \nL130: Filmmaker and editor at Stark Insider, covering arts, AI & tech, and indie film. Inspired by Bergman, slow cinema and Chipotle. Often found behind the camera or in the edit bay. Peloton: ClintTheMint.\nL131: \nL132:   * cite0†CULTURE L133:   * cite24†BEST OF AI L134:   * cite1†FILMMAKING/TECH L135:   * cite2†ATELIER STARK FILMS†atelierstark.com L136:   * cite25†HUMANxAI SYMBIOSIS L137: \nL138: THE STARK COLLECTIVE\nL139: \nL140:   * cite26†THE STARK CO†thestark.co L141:   * cite27†STARK INSIDER L142:   * cite28†STARKMIND†starkmind.ai L143:   * cite29†ATELIER STARK†atelierstark.com L144: © Copyright 2005-2026 BLG Media LLC. v2.20.0\nL145: \nL146:   * cite30†Review Policy and Shipping L147:   * cite31†Privacy Policy L148:   * cite32†Contact L149:   * cite33†About --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMeta Muse gives you a pretty nice VM for free (https://unexcitedneurons.substack.com/p/meta-muse-gives-you-a-pretty-nice)\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn0search5\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 60\nL0: # cite0†unexcitedneurons’s Substack L1: # Meta Muse gives you a pretty nice VM for free\nL2: \nL3: cite1†unexcitedneurons†substack.com L4: \nL5: Sep 30, 2026\nL6: \nL7: Share\nL8: \nL9: When Zuck launched cite2†Muse†about.fb.com on September 8, most of the news focused on the agent. I got curious about the VM that it runs on. Every Muse agent gets its own Muse Secure VM, which is supposed to be a dedicated VM for each user, where your agent and data lives. This is available to every free user (not just paying users).\nL10: So I decided to ask what it is running on, and it turns out to be :\nL11:   * 2 vCPUs, clock at 1497.749 MHz, sharing 1 physical core\nL12: \nL13:   * AMD EPYC 9D25 126-Core Processor\nL14: \nL15:   * KVM (hypervisor flag set in /proc/cpuinfo)\nL16: \nL17:   * 8GB of RAM\nL18: \nL19:   * 100GB volume for home, plus 7.5GB mounted at /\nL20: \nL21:   * About 644 MB/s read, 177 MB/s write\nL22: \nL23:   * Ubuntu 24.04, Linux kernel 7.0\nL24: \nL25: This is actually… quite great, especially for free. This would be a $10-20/month VPS at a typical provider. DigitalOcean would charge you $40/month for a similar machine.\nL26: ### Does Meta throttle the machine?\nL27: \nL28: No.\nL29: \nL30: Meta gives you full power as long as you need it. Unlike Amazon burstable instances (T2/T3/etc), I benchmarked the VM for over an hour, and never found any throttling.\nL31: \nL32: I ran 2 workers for an hour minutes, logging throughput every second alongside CPU steal from /proc/stat. The first 30 seconds averaged 3,411,111 iterations/s; the last 30 seconds averaged 3,280,000. You get full power all the time, with no burst and no throttle.\nL33: Then I ran sysbench with 2 threads, and got only 1.44x the single-threaded score. That was ambiguous; it could be just constantly throttling me (instead of a burst throttle), or it could be 2 threads running via SMT on the same core.\nL34: \nL35: So I ran OpenSSL SHA512 and AES-256 benchmarks, and got 1.98x and 2x respectively. This shows the 2 vCPUs are the 2 SMT threads of a single physical EPYC core, running at about 1.5 GHz.\nL36: ## For free, it’s definitely nice\nL37: \nL38: It’s just like having your own VPS box, with no shutdowns and no throttling. I saw no evidence that the VM was oversubscribed on the host. I’m sure it is, because I heavily doubt Meta would only put 126 VMs on a single server, but I never hit any limits.\nL39: \nL40: Share\nL41: \nL42: #### Discussion about this post\nL43: \nL44: [Button: Comments][Button: Restacks]\nL45: \nL46: cite3†Image: User's avatar†substackcdn.com L47: \nL48: No posts\nL49: ### Ready for more?\nL50: \nL51: [Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input][Input]\nL52: \nL53: [Input: Type your email...]\nL54: \nL55: © 2026 unexcitedneurons · cite4†Privacy†substack.com ∙ cite5†Terms†substack.com ∙ cite6†Collection notice†substack.com L56: \nL57: cite7†Start your Substack†substack.com cite8†Get the app†substack.com L58: \nL59: cite9†Substack†substack.com is the home for great culture\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExclusive: Meta’s Muse Surpassed 500,000 Users After First Week — The Information (https://www.theinformation.com/briefings/exclusive-metas-muse-surpassed-500-000-users-first-week)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 25] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.theinformation.com/briefings/exclusive-metas-muse-surpassed-500-000-users-first-week\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 181\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nExclusive: Meta’s Muse Tops 3 Million Weekly Users — The Information (https://www.theinformation.com/briefings/exclusive-metas-muse-tops-3-million-weekly-users)\nciteturn1view4 [wordlim: 25] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.theinformation.com/briefings/exclusive-metas-muse-tops-3-million-weekly-users\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 181\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn1view5 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://au.finance.yahoo.com/news/meta-bulls-have-a-new-muse-stat-to-love-amazon-could-spoil-it-165920814.html/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAMD EPYC™ 9965 (https://www.amd.com/en/products/processors/server/epyc/9005-series/amd-epyc-9965.html)\nciteturn1view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.amd.com/en/products/processors/server/epyc/9005-series/amd-epyc-9965.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 608\n"
